# interplay_rand_33: Seeded random guest programs (pgtest/randgen.py): each seed is one scenario, interplay_rand_<seed>, whose program the generator builds from the seed alone.  A program is a walk over legal guest and host actions across every subsystem: tasks and their objects made and deleted, blit, compute, render and info work on the root FIFO and child FIFOs (serialized by barriers, or at once on different tasks), display presents, cursor moves and hot plugs, IOSurface maps, tests and swaps, pause and unpause, suspend and resume on a fresh device, child FIFOs deleted and defined again, and reset, stop or a fresh device with the guest doing its set-up again. After every step the guest can observe, the scenario waits for it; checkpoints are guest memory, registers and event counts, never timing.  The check is differential: before the walk a control task runs every item of work of the program, uninterrupted, into control slots on the pristine device; after each item of the walk its slot is compared with its control slot, so a lifecycle call or a concurrent FIFO that changes what the GPU computed shows as a cmp that is not equal. Each distinct kind of work's control result is dumped once. The setups a program runs in follow from what it uses: info replies abort an in-process host, so a program with info work runs in isolate only; the IOSurface host device needs a memory map (map and isolate).  What the walks found, and the model now keeps: deleting child FIFO n zeroes node n+1's page list (W3), and every unpause starts every child FIFO again, mapping its page list again, just as a resume does. So a live child n+1 stops at the next pause and unpause (or rvg_device_will_suspend and unpause) as well as at a resume: its node still says running, a NOP rung on it never runs, and a barrier on its stamp blocks the FIFO that waits. The programs lock it where it happens ("a NOP is not run"), and the guest recovers the child by writing its page list again, deleting it and defining it.  This program: ports=2, teardown, scratch, plug, held; 52 steps: check 1, child_del 2, child_new 3, held 5, hotplug 1, pause 3, present 3, scratch_del 1, scratch_new 5, suspend 2, task_del 3, task_new 3, teardown 2, unplug 3, work 15.
